# Express App Factory Pattern

## Rule

Never export a module-level singleton app or instantiate services/repositories at module scope. Always use a `createApp(deps)` factory so every caller (including tests) gets fresh, isolated instances.

## Structure

```typescript
// app.ts
export interface AppDependencies {
  appRepository?: AppRepository;
  trafficRepository?: TrafficRepository;
  rateLimitStore?: MemoryStore;
  // add new deps here, never at module scope
}

export function createApp(deps: AppDependencies = {}): Application {
  const appRepo = deps.appRepository ?? new AppRepository();
  const trafficRepo = deps.trafficRepository ?? new TrafficRepository();
  const rateLimitStore = deps.rateLimitStore ?? new MemoryStore();

  const service = new TrafficService(trafficRepo, appRepo);
  const controller = new TrafficController(service);
  // ...
  return app;
}
```

```typescript
// server.ts (entrypoint only)
import { createApp } from './app';
const app = createApp();
app.listen(3000);
```

## Rules

- `app.ts` exports `createApp(deps)` and `AppDependencies` — nothing else
- Services receive all repositories via constructor, never `new Repo()` inside the service
- Repositories have a `clear()` method for test teardown
- `server.ts` is the only file that calls `createApp()` with no arguments

## Anti-patterns

```typescript
// ❌ module-level singleton
const app = express();
export default app;

// ❌ service creates its own dependencies
export class TrafficService {
  private repo = new TrafficRepository(); // NO
}

// ❌ singleton exports
export const trafficService = new TrafficService();
```

## Test pattern

```typescript
let appRepo: AppRepository;
let app: Application;

beforeEach(() => {
  appRepo = new AppRepository();
  app = createApp({ appRepository: appRepo });
});
```
